.TH SPEAK 1
.SH NAME
speak \- read something out
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B speak
[
.B -l
.I lexicon
]
[
.B -p
.I pitch
]
[
.B -s
.I speed
]
[
.B -u
.I units
]
[
.B -f
]
[
.B -o
.I file
]
[
.B -q
]
[
.B -t
.I text
]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Speak
reads its input out loud, a line at a time.
Each utterance goes through
.IR voice (2),
the same neural voice
.IR say (1)
narrates with - when it can, and through
.IR voice (3),
the formant synthesizer, when it cannot: no room for sixty megabytes of model,
no
.IR ml (3),
or
.BR -f ,
which skips even trying.
With no
.I file
it reads standard input, so it composes:
.IP
.EX
ls /dev | speak
speak /tmp/notes
speak -t 'the disk is full'
speak /dev/voice/screen &	# the screen reader
man2speak /man/1/paste | sentences | speak	# a man page, read like prose
.EE
.PP
That fourth one is the whole screen reader.
.PP
The last one matters for anything that reads like a paragraph rather
than a directory listing.
.I Speak
paces itself one input line at a time, which is exactly right for
.B "ls /dev"
and exactly wrong for a man page that
.IR man2txt (1)
has wrapped at a fixed column with no regard for where a sentence ends -
piped straight through, it pauses mid-sentence and runs through the
period at the end of the next one.
.IR Sentences (1)
reflows wrapped text back into one sentence per line first, so the
pauses land where a person reading it aloud would put them, and
.IR man2speak (1)
does the same parsing
.I man2txt
does but keeps a section heading out of the paragraph that follows it,
which
.I sentences
has no way to recover once it's gone.
.IR Voice (3)'s
.B screen
file gives one announcement per line and blocks until there is one, so reading
it out loud is what this program already does.
.PP
.B -p
and
.B -s
set the pitch in Hz and the speed as a percentage; both are
.IR voice (3)'s,
and apply only to what
.I that
voice says - whatever falls back to it, or everything, under
.BR -f .
.B -u
is
.IR ml (3)'s
compute units for the neural voice, "cpu" by default; see
.IR say (1)
for why.
.B -o
writes the samples to a file instead of
.BR /dev/audio ,
which is how this gets tested without listening to it.
.B -q
stops it reporting words it could not say, or a fallback taking place.
.B -n
prints the sounds and says nothing, which is how any of this gets checked
without listening to it - and skips opening the neural voice at all, since a
dry run never speaks either one.
.PP
A line is not begun until the one before it has been heard, so a pipe paces
itself rather than queueing everything at once and talking over itself.
.PP
When the input is
.BR /dev/voice/screen ,
a line already being said is abandoned as soon as a person's own action queues
something newer, and the sound already written is flushed \(em otherwise
closing the audio device would wait for it, which is the whole problem.
A reader that has to finish telling you about the last screen before it can
tell you about this one is unusable.
Output does not interrupt output; see
.IR voice (3).
.SH "WHY NOT JUST SAY(1)"
.IR Say (1)
speaks one utterance and exits, having loaded sixty megabytes of model to do
it \(em between one and two seconds whatever is being said.
That is right for narration and useless for reading: nothing can be piped into
it, and a second and a half a line is not reading, it is waiting.
.PP
.IR Voice (2)
is the same model, held open rather than loaded fresh per utterance, the way
.I say
itself now holds it: the first utterance still costs the load, but every one
after that costs tens of milliseconds, which is fast enough to read with.
So this uses it by default \(em the screen someone hears about should sound
like the same voice
.I say
narrates a tutorial with, not a different one.
.PP
.IR Voice (3),
the formant synthesizer, is what this falls back to: for the whole run if the
neural voice could not be opened at all, or for one line if it refuses that
line specifically \(em it has no equivalent of skipping one unknown symbol the
way
.IR voice (3)
does, so an unknown phoneme there is a whole utterance the neural voice will
not attempt. It sounds much worse and is far faster to fail into, which is
what a fallback is for: a screen reader must not go silent because the better
voice is not available, or is pickier about one line than the rest.
.B -f
skips the neural voice unconditionally, for reading anywhere the extra memory
is not worth it, or the first utterance costing a model-load's worth of
silence is not acceptable.
.SH "WHAT IT DOES TO A LINE"
The first thing this was given to read was
.B ls /dev
and it said nothing at all.
Every line is a path,
.B /dev/cons
is one token with no spaces in it, and no dictionary has an entry for
.BR devcons .
Silence, from the most ordinary output there is.
.PP
So slash, dot, dash, underscore and the other characters that hold identifiers
together become spaces, which turns a path into the words it is made of, and
digits become their names, because
.B 4
is not a word and an error code is exactly the thing being read out.
.PP
A word that is in neither the lexicon nor the dictionary is spelled out, letter
by letter, and the rest of the line is still said.
That is the opposite of
.IR say (1)'s
choice and deliberate: there the words are chosen in advance and a refusal is a
correction waiting to be made, here they arrive unbidden and silence is a
failure at the one thing this is for.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/speak.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR sentences (1),
.IR man2speak (1),
.IR voice (2),
.IR phon (2),
.IR voice (3),
.IR audio (3)
.SH BUGS
Falling back to
.IR voice (3)
mid-run, one line at a time, means a single session can be heard switching
between two different-sounding voices - whenever the neural voice refuses a
line the formant one would have skipped a symbol in instead.
That is deliberate, not an oversight: see "WHY NOT JUST SAY(1)" above.
Nothing here decides which voice to use ahead of time by looking at the line;
it only finds out by trying.
.PP
Spelling is exact and slow.
.B hoststdin
is nine letters read out one at a time, where letter-to-sound rules would give
a word-shaped approximation in a fraction of the time.
.IR Phon (2)
has no rules; this is where their absence is felt.
.PP
Splitting identifiers into the words they are made of was tried and removed.
It is the obvious idea \(em
.B hoststdin
ought to come apart into
.BR host ,
.B std
and
.B in
\(em and what it produced was
.BR "hosts td in" ,
because the longest prefix that is a word is
.B hosts
and
.B td
is in the dictionary too.
Both readings have three pieces, so counting them does not choose between them,
and the wrong one sounds exactly as confident as the right one.
Spelling cannot be wrong.
.PP
Interruption only applies when reading
.BR /dev/voice/screen ,
and only at a tenth-of-a-second boundary.
A file or a pipe is read to the end, because nothing is going to supersede it,
and there is still no way to stop that short of killing it.
.PP
Nothing here has been listened to by whoever wrote it.
